# E546 draft — Header declares sig_atomic_t but omits <signal.h>
id:                     E546
trigger_tool:           clang|make|conan
phase:                  compilation
error_signature:        ircd_signal\.h:.*unknown type name 'sig_atomic_t'|error: unknown type name 'sig_atomic_t'

severity:               2
category:               portability, libc, headers, musl, ohos
match_keywords:         sig_atomic_t, signal.h, unknown type name, self-contained header, transitive include, include order, OHOS libc
title:                  Header using sig_atomic_t must include signal.h

root_cause:             src/ircd_signal.h uses sig_atomic_t without including <signal.h>, the standard header that declares this type. An unrelated header may expose the typedef transitively on one libc or under one include order, masking the omission. The OHOS Clang build reached this header without that incidental declaration and failed with unknown type name 'sig_atomic_t'. A header must include the owner of each type it uses so it remains compilable when included first.
affected_scope:         C/C++ projects whose own headers use POSIX types or declarations but rely on unrelated headers to include the owning standard header transitively; the defect is exposed when OHOS libc or a changed include order removes that accidental dependency.
verification_cmd:       conan create archives/i/ircd-hybrid/8.2.46-dfsg.1 -pr:h=ci/conan/profiles/ohos-aarch64 -pr:b=ci/conan/profiles/ohos-aarch64 --build=missing
rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           include-type-owning-header-in-owner
rec_description:        Add an unconditional #include <signal.h> to the header that uses sig_atomic_t, then compile that header as the first include in a minimal translation unit and rebuild the package.
rec_command:            Add #include <signal.h> to src/ircd_signal.h; run the OHOS Conan create command from verification_cmd.
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               source-patch

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      include-owner-at-every-consumer
fallback_description:   If the upstream header cannot be changed, include <signal.h> before it in every consuming translation unit. This is temporary because it leaves the header non-self-contained and order-sensitive.
fallback_command:
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          source-patch

forbid_1_pattern:       (typedef|#define)\s+sig_atomic_t|(-D|-include).*sig_atomic_t
forbid_1_reason:        Redefining the POSIX type or injecting a command-line alias hides the missing owner header and can give the signal flag an incompatible type or inconsistent definition across translation units.
forbid_1_consequence:   The package may compile with a non-conforming signal flag type while the original include-order defect remains for other consumers.
forbid_2_pattern:       -include[ =].*signal\.h
forbid_2_reason:        A forced include changes every translation unit and masks the broken header contract instead of fixing it at the declaration site.
forbid_2_consequence:   Consumers outside this build can still fail depending on include order.

principles:             P010
causes:                 []
caused_by:              []
similar_to:             [E420, E721, E634-draft-62B2]
conflicts_with:         []
prerequisite_of:        []

doc_file:               knowledge/drafts/E546-draft-A54E.md
last_updated:           2026-10-03
source_event:           ircd-hybrid/8.2.46-dfsg.1 HarmonyOS aarch64 build: src/ircd_signal.h fails because sig_atomic_t is undeclared; patch 0002 adds the type-owning standard header and the final OHOS package build passes.
